<Project>

  <!--
    Single source of truth for the application version.

    The repo-root VERSION file is read here and applied to EVERY .NET project: MSBuild imports the
    nearest Directory.Build.props automatically for all projects beneath this directory, so setting
    <Version> here flows into AssemblyVersion / FileVersion / InformationalVersion of each assembly
    (including ModularCA.API, whose VersionController reports it at runtime).

    The SAME VERSION file is read by the frontends' vite.config.ts (injected as __APP_VERSION__), so
    the backend and the UI always report the identical version.

    To bump the version, edit ./VERSION ONLY. A value like "0.1.0-rc2" is parsed by MSBuild as
    VersionPrefix=0.1.0 + VersionSuffix=rc2: AssemblyVersion/FileVersion become 0.1.0.0 (numeric, as
    required) while InformationalVersion keeps the full "0.1.0-rc2".
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ModularCaVersionRaw>$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)VERSION'))</ModularCaVersionRaw>
    <Version>$(ModularCaVersionRaw.Trim())</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The Staging configuration.

    Staging exists for one reason: it is Release plus JavaScript sourcemaps. ModularCA.API.csproj
    sets MODULARCA_SOURCEMAP=1 when Configuration is Staging, each vite.config.ts reads it, and
    scripts/build-linux-dist.sh packages the result when given that configuration. A minified stack like
    `te.map is not a function` at `index-DbKwh5kT.js:14` costs most of a day to trace on a deployed
    host; a filename and a line number costs minutes.

    These two properties are what keep the rest of the build identical. The SDK applies its Release
    defaults by testing `'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'` literally, so an unrecognised configuration
    name silently inherits the *Debug* side of those defaults instead — Optimize would be false and
    TRACE would be undefined, and a "staging" artifact would then carry unoptimized IL while looking
    for all the world like a release build. Set them explicitly so the only difference that reaches
    the host is the .map files.

    Deliberately NOT a solution configuration: ModularCA.sln lists Debug and Release only. Staging is
    a packaging concern, driven by the build script publishing the two projects directly, and adding
    it to the solution would put a third option in front of every developer in the IDE to no purpose.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Staging'">
    <Optimize>true</Optimize>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);TRACE</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Supply-chain vulnerability gate (NuGet audit).

    Rationale: a certificate authority is a trust anchor. A known-vulnerable dependency shipping in a
    CA build is a materially worse outcome than a red build, so advisories must STOP the pipeline
    rather than scroll past in the warning noise. This block was added after finding F-04, where
    Microsoft.OpenApi 2.4.1 (GHSA-v5pm-xwqc-g5wc, HIGH) emitted NU1903 on every single compile for an
    extended period and nobody noticed, because a warning among hundreds of warnings is invisible.

    What each setting does, and why it is set the way it is:

      NuGetAudit=true
        Turns on NuGet's restore-time check of every resolved package against the GitHub Advisory
        Database (served through the nuget.org registration feed). This is the SDK default today, but
        it is stated explicitly so that a future SDK default-flip, or a stray global property, cannot
        silently disable the gate. Security controls should never depend on an implicit default.

      NuGetAuditMode=all
        The SDK default is "direct", which only audits packages we reference by name. That is exactly
        the wrong scope for us: the overwhelming majority of a .NET app's attack surface arrives
        transitively (Swashbuckle.AspNetCore, for example, is what drags Microsoft.OpenApi in). "all"
        audits the entire resolved graph, direct and transitive alike.

      NuGetAuditLevel=low
        Report EVERYTHING — low, moderate, high, critical. Note this is the REPORTING threshold, not
        the failure threshold; the two are deliberately decoupled below. We want full visibility of
        every advisory touching the graph, including low-severity ones we may consciously choose to
        accept, because "we never saw it" is not an acceptable answer during an audit. Verified: at
        this threshold, with mode=all, the solution currently restores with ZERO audit warnings, so
        turning the dial all the way down costs us nothing today.

      WarningsAsErrors += NU1903;NU1904
        The failure threshold. NuGet emits one warning code per severity band:
          NU1901 = low, NU1902 = moderate, NU1903 = high, NU1904 = critical.
        Only HIGH and CRITICAL are promoted to build-breaking errors. Low and moderate advisories stay
        visible as warnings so they can be triaged on their own schedule. This asymmetry is
        intentional: moderate advisories against transitive dependencies are common and often have no
        fixed version available yet, and a gate that fires constantly for un-actionable reasons gets
        disabled by the first developer it inconveniences. A gate that only fires on high/critical
        stays credible, and those are the ones that genuinely must not ship.

        NU1900 (audit could not reach the advisory source) is deliberately NOT promoted — it fires on
        offline or air-gapped builds, which is an availability problem, not a vulnerability.

    Escape hatch: if a high-severity advisory has no fix and the build must proceed, suppress that ONE
    package with a scoped <NoWarn>NU1903</NoWarn> on its PackageReference and a comment recording the
    advisory ID and the accepted risk. Do not weaken this file — the blast radius is every project.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
